## The Infant Who Notices

The first stage of learning is not remembering.

It is detecting.

The newborn nervous system is already organized enough to distinguish patterns in light, sound, touch, movement, and bodily state. This organization is biological, but it is not finished.

Vision provides a useful example.

Newborn vision is limited compared with adult vision, yet infants are sensitive to contrast, motion, and structured visual patterns. They preferentially orient toward some kinds of configurations, including face-like arrangements. Hearing is similarly active from the beginning. Infants are sensitive to speech rhythm and to distinctions between sounds that adults may no longer notice.

These abilities are not complete representations of people, objects, or language.

They are starting conditions.

The distinction matters.

Sensation is not perception.

Perceptual discrimination is not object representation.

Object representation is not meaning.

And a detectable difference is not necessarily an understood difference.

A newborn may detect that two sounds differ without knowing what produced them. An older infant may represent an object behind an occluder while still failing to retrieve it manually. Cross-modal matching may show that information from two senses can be coordinated without implying a fully integrated theory of the world.

Development is therefore not the simple addition of information to an empty system.

The system itself is changing.

---

## When the Brain Specializes

One of the clearest demonstrations of the interaction between biology and experience is perceptual narrowing.

Early in life, infants possess unusually broad perceptual sensitivities. They can discriminate some speech contrasts that occur in languages they have never heard and can distinguish categories of faces that adults process less efficiently.

Then experience begins to change the system.

With repeated exposure to the language of the surrounding community, infants become increasingly specialized for the sound distinctions that matter in that linguistic environment. Comparable specialization occurs in face processing and other perceptual domains.

At first this can sound like a story of loss.

It is better understood as calibration.

A nervous system that remained equally sensitive to every possible distinction would be extraordinarily expensive to operate. Specialization allows the brain to devote greater efficiency to the patterns it encounters repeatedly and are therefore likely to matter.

The infant begins broad.

Experience makes perception specific.

That is not deterioration.

It is adaptation.

And it reveals something important about development: experience does not merely fill a pre-existing mind. Experience changes the perceptual machinery through which later information will be processed.

But even here, timing must not be turned into destiny.

Sensitive periods are windows in which particular forms of experience and maturation interact especially strongly. They are not universal deadlines after which learning becomes impossible.

The evidence for perceptual narrowing illustrates this beautifully. Later experience can, under some conditions, restore or increase sensitivity to distinctions that had become less readily processed. The system becomes specialized, but it does not become a sealed box.

Development remains plastic.

---

## Attention: The Gatekeeper

The world contains more information than an infant can process at once.

Something therefore has to be selected.

Attention is that selection process.

Infants do not distribute processing equally across every available signal. Novelty, movement, social information, contrast, and other properties can attract attention. Repeated information can become less compelling after it has been encoded. Attention itself is not one ability but a family of processes involving orienting, disengagement, sustained attention, and increasing voluntary control.

This makes attention a developmental bottleneck.

Information that is never effectively selected cannot easily become part of later learning.

But attention should not be treated as a personality trait or moral quality.

A tired infant may look inattentive.

A hungry infant may disengage.

A distressed infant may struggle to sustain attention.

A highly stimulating environment may produce more movement and less useful processing rather than better learning.

The important question is not simply whether an infant can attend.

It is whether the infant can sustain the appropriate kind of attention under the conditions in which real learning occurs.

This is another point where the previous chapter matters.

Regulation and cognition are not separate departments.

A regulated infant has different opportunities for attention from a distressed one. The body helps determine what the mind can process.

Attention therefore sits between physiology and learning.

It is the gate through which experience becomes information.

