## Finding Patterns in the Noise

Once information is selected, the infant can begin searching for regularities.

This capacity is known as statistical learning.

Human language provides one of the clearest examples.

Spoken language does not arrive with spaces between words. An adult listener effortlessly hears separate units within continuous sound. An infant has to discover those units.

In a classic experiment, infants listened to continuous streams of speech-like syllables with no pauses separating the hidden words. The only reliable clues were statistical: syllables occurring within a word were more likely to follow one another than syllables spanning a word boundary.

After a remarkably brief exposure, infants distinguished the higher-probability sequences from lower-probability combinations.

The significance of the result is larger than the artificial experiment itself.

The infant does not need every structure to be explicitly taught.

The structure can sometimes be extracted from repeated experience.

Similar statistical learning has been observed across auditory and visual domains. This suggests that infants possess powerful mechanisms for identifying regularities in sequences and distributions.

But “statistical learner” should not become another mythological label.

Statistical learning can detect structure.

It does not by itself explain goals, social meaning, causal understanding, memory consolidation, cultural norms, or the development of language as a symbolic system.

Infant learning is plural.

Associative learning, contingency learning, imitation, observational learning, reinforcement, statistical learning, and socially guided learning overlap and interact.

There is no need to make one mechanism the winner.

The more interesting question is how these mechanisms cooperate.

