## Prediction: Learning What Comes Next

Statistical regularity becomes especially important when it generates expectations.

Contemporary developmental neuroscience increasingly uses predictive models to describe how the nervous system processes incoming information. In simplified form, the brain generates expectations about what is likely to happen and compares incoming sensory information against those expectations. When the prediction is confirmed, processing can become more efficient. When the input differs substantially from expectation, the discrepancy can drive further processing and learning.

This provides a useful bridge between learning and prediction.

A repeated event becomes expected.

An unexpected event attracts attention.

Attention creates an opportunity for updating.

The cycle repeats.

This does not mean that an infant consciously thinks:

*I predicted X, but observed Y, therefore my generative model requires revision.*

The machinery can operate without conscious theorizing.

This distinction is essential.

To say that an infant forms predictions is not to say that the infant possesses adult-like beliefs. It means that the nervous system becomes sensitive to regularities and uses previous information to shape its response to new input.

The infant is not consciously writing equations.

The infant is learning what usually comes next.

---

## Remembering What Happened

Prediction requires history.

The present can only be compared with the past if something about the past has been retained.

Infant memory is therefore one of the strongest arguments against the image of the cognitively empty newborn.

But memory is not a single system.

Researchers distinguish among recognition, procedural and implicit learning, working memory, conditioned responses, and forms of recall. These systems develop on different schedules and are affected by age, task, context, and retrieval conditions.

One of the most influential demonstrations came from the mobile-conjugate reinforcement paradigm.

An infant is placed beneath a mobile. A ribbon connects the infant's leg to the mobile. The infant discovers that kicking causes movement.

Later, the infant is returned to the same setting.

If kicking increases, the infant has retained information about the earlier contingency.

Research using this paradigm showed that very young infants can retain learned associations, with retention becoming longer and more specific with age.

This is important because it separates three questions that are often collapsed into one:

**Was something encoded?**

**Was it retained?**

**Could it be retrieved under the conditions of the test?**

An infant may remember something without being able to demonstrate that memory in every context.

A changed room can matter.

A changed cue can matter.

A different motor demand can matter.

Failure to retrieve is therefore not proof that no representation existed.

This distinction becomes crucial when interpreting infantile amnesia later in development. Early memory and later autobiographical memory are not the same system.

The infant can remember before the child can tell a story about remembering.

