## Building a World

The infant must eventually learn not only that patterns recur, but that the world has structure.

Objects appear and disappear.

Things move.

Surfaces support other things.

Actions produce consequences.

People behave in ways that can be more or less predictable.

This is where the famous history of object permanence becomes useful.

Piaget's early work placed an enormous emphasis on action. The child had to coordinate perception, memory, and movement to solve increasingly difficult problems. Later looking-time experiments appeared to demonstrate object knowledge at much younger ages.

The disagreement helped reveal an important principle.

Different tasks measure different layers of competence.

An infant might expect an object to persist but lack the motor control to search for it correctly.

An infant might represent where an object should be while still being pulled toward a previously reinforced reaching location.

An apparent cognitive error may therefore emerge partly from the interaction of memory, attention, motor history, posture, and task demands.

Dynamic Systems Theory made this point especially clearly. Behavior can be “softly assembled” from interacting systems rather than produced by a single internal controller. Neural maturation, muscle strength, body proportions, physical forces, memory, perception, and experience all participate in the behavior that finally appears.

The mind is embodied.

A baby's cognition does not float above the body.

It is built through the body's encounters with the world.

---

## Agency: Discovering That Action Matters

The next discovery is even more intimate.

The infant begins to learn that some events are connected to what the infant does.

A kick moves the mobile.

A reach brings an object closer.

A vocalization may bring another person's attention.

A gesture may produce a response.

Contingency learning provides the beginnings of agency: the sensitivity to relationships between one's own actions and their consequences.

From there, development becomes increasingly complex.

The infant learns that actions can be purposeful.

Other people act toward goals.

Objects behave according to constraints.

Events can be predicted.

Causes can be distinguished from coincidences.

But these stages should not be confused with adult causal reasoning.

Sensitivity to a contingency is not the same as a mature theory of causation.

The progression is better understood as gradual differentiation:

**regularity → contingency → agency → prediction → increasingly explicit causal representation**

Language and action eventually allow these representations to become far more flexible and explicit.

The infant first discovers that the world has patterns.

Then that some patterns have causes.

Then that some causes can be manipulated.

The scientist is not born fully formed.

The scientist is assembled.

---

## Other Minds Become Part of the Environment

The infant is never learning from objects alone.

Human beings are among the most informative structures in the environment.

Faces attract attention.

Voices matter.

Gaze matters.

Gestures matter.

Expressions matter.

And unlike most physical objects, people respond.

That responsiveness creates a special form of learning.

A caregiver looks toward an object.

The infant follows the gaze.

The caregiver points.

The infant looks.

The caregiver names the object.

The infant hears the sound while seeing its likely referent.

Information from two minds begins to converge around the same thing.

This is the beginning of shared attention.

Joint attention is a major developmental achievement because the infant is no longer attending only to an object. The infant is learning about another person's attention to that object.

This creates a bridge from individual learning to shared meaning.

Social information is therefore not merely another sensory channel.

It organizes the interpretation of other channels.

A caregiver's face can alter the meaning of an unfamiliar object.

A caregiver's voice can direct attention.

A caregiver's response can tell the infant whether an action had an effect.

A caregiver's gesture can narrow a vast field of possible information to a single relevant feature.

Social learning is therefore deeply relational.

The mind develops in a social world because other people continually help organize the information that enters it.

---

## The Caregiver Does Not Install a Mind

There is an important difference between shaping an environment and programming a child.

Caregivers do not install knowledge.

They create opportunities in which learning can occur.

A responsive adult who notices an infant's attention and follows it provides repeated, contingent information.

A caregiver who talks during ordinary activities exposes the infant to the statistical structure of language.

A shared game creates repetition with small variations.

Peek-a-boo contains both predictability and surprise.

A familiar routine allows the infant to anticipate what comes next.

A new texture, movement, object, or sound allows the infant to explore what differs.

This is scaffolding in its broadest sense.

It does not require formal teaching.

It requires an environment that is sufficiently rich to provide useful information and sufficiently coherent for patterns to be discoverable.

That distinction matters because modern parents are often offered a distorted version of enrichment.

More cards.

More classes.

More sounds.

More toys.

More stimulation.

More optimization.

But more information is not automatically better information.

The infant's learning systems depend on patterns, not indiscriminate sensory quantity. An environment that becomes chaotic can increase noise rather than increase learning opportunity. The goal is not maximal stimulation.

It is meaningful experience.

Routine is not the enemy of learning.

For an infant, repetition is data.

---

## The First Curriculum Is Everyday Life

The most powerful learning environment may therefore be the least theatrical.

A parent speaking while preparing food.

A caregiver naming the object the baby is reaching toward.

A song repeated every evening.

A familiar face appearing after a brief absence.

A book read repeatedly.

A walk in which the same street is encountered from a different physical position.

A game played again and again, with small variations.

These ordinary experiences give the infant something that a flashier environment may not: structured regularity.

The dossier's practical framework follows this principle. Statistical learning makes repetition meaningful. Predictive processing makes small variations informative. Perceptual narrowing makes exposure to diverse people and languages developmentally relevant. Dynamic Systems Theory makes varied physical experience useful because the infant's motor system must continuously solve changing problems.

The implication is reassuring.

A caregiver does not need to become an educational technician.

The infant already possesses the machinery for learning.

What matters is giving that machinery an environment in which it can work.

---

## The Brain That Changes

All of this learning is occurring inside a nervous system undergoing enormous biological change.

Neural development involves changing patterns of synaptic connectivity, myelination, network specialization, and experience-dependent plasticity.

Plasticity is often presented as an invitation to optimism.

It should also be understood as a source of vulnerability.

A plastic system can adapt.

But a plastic system is also capable of adapting to poor conditions.

Experience can alter developmental probabilities and system organization. It does not follow that every experience permanently rewires the child, nor that every early difference has a lifelong consequence.

This is where developmental neuroscience most needs restraint.

A synapse is not a personality.

A brain-volume difference is not an intelligence score.

A neural response is not a diagnosis.

A period of rapid synaptic development is not a universal critical window.

The brain is changing continuously, and different systems have different schedules.

Sensitive periods should therefore be understood as windows of heightened interaction between maturation and experience, not as universal deadlines after which development is closed.

The phrase “use it or lose it” is too simple.

Human development is more forgiving, more complicated, and more variable than that.

---

## No Two Minds Take the Same Route

The same developmental machinery operates differently in different children.

Infants vary in reactivity, approach, activity, affect, attention, and regulation. Some differences show short-term stability. Others change substantially with maturation and context.

The developmental pathway is therefore not a single staircase.

It is a family of possible routes.

A child may reach one skill earlier and another later.

One infant may attend intensely to people while another is more absorbed by objects.

One may be highly reactive to novelty; another may approach new experiences readily.

A difference in performance may reflect temperament, health, sensory experience, motor opportunity, language environment, culture, fatigue, context, or the design of the task itself.

This is why early difference must never be casually translated into failure.

Nor should developmental diversity be reduced to a single deficit model.

The scientific task is to understand which differences are stable, which are plastic, which reflect meaningful support needs, and which simply reflect ordinary variation.

The practical task is different.

It is to give children enough opportunity for development without demanding that every child follow the same timetable.

Development is dynamic.

Variation is part of the system.

