## When Infant Science Learned to Doubt Itself

The history of infant cognition contains a useful paradox.

Researchers became better at detecting early abilities at the same time that they became more vulnerable to overinterpreting those abilities.

Violation-of-expectation research was revolutionary because it allowed researchers to study infants without requiring advanced motor skills. An infant could look longer at an event that violated a physical expectation, apparently revealing knowledge that earlier action-based tasks had failed to detect.

The temptation was immediate.

Perhaps babies understood physics.

Perhaps they understood numbers.

Perhaps they understood morality.

But the inference chain was longer than it appeared.

An “impossible” event may look different for reasons unrelated to the proposed concept.

Novelty can attract attention.

Changes in edges, motion, or visual structure can alter looking.

Familiarity can affect response.

A simple behavioral measure can therefore support several possible interpretations.

The lesson is not that violation-of-expectation research is useless.

The lesson is that the richer the interpretation, the more evidence is required.

The ManyBabies collaboration illustrates what this looks like in practice.

Some infant findings have survived large-scale replication. The preference for infant-directed speech, for example, has shown robustness across multisite research. Other influential findings have not. In the widely cited helper-versus-hinderer paradigm, a large ManyBabies replication involving more than a thousand infants across dozens of laboratories did not reproduce the original strong preference for helpful agents; the observed preference was essentially at chance.

This is not a failure of science.

It is science doing its job.

A claim became famous.

Researchers tested it more rigorously.

The effect weakened.

The theory had to change.

Developmental science therefore teaches two lessons at once:

Infants are more capable learners than older theories assumed.

Scientists are less capable of reading infant minds from isolated behaviors than those same theories sometimes implied.

Both statements can be true.

---

## What We Cannot Infer

This distinction should accompany almost every extraordinary claim about early development.

Looking longer does not automatically mean deeper understanding.

A preference does not automatically mean a conscious judgment.

Early neural activity does not automatically predict later intelligence.

A sensitive period is not a developmental deadline.

A group difference is not an individual forecast.

A temporary physiological response is not necessarily permanent programming.

A developmental delay is not automatically developmental failure.

And a difference in a child's developmental pathway is not evidence of deficient parenting.

The field's methodological caution is therefore not an appendix to the science.

It is part of the science.

The question is never merely whether an effect exists.

It is:

**What does the evidence actually identify?**

