## The Developmental Map

From birth through the second year, the broad movement is visible even though individual trajectories vary.

In the earliest months, infants display broad sensory sensitivities and begin extracting regularities from experience. They can retain simple learned contingencies, and their actions are initially constrained by immature control systems.

Over the following months, visual scanning becomes more sophisticated, cross-modal coordination increases, reaching becomes more goal-directed, and memory becomes more durable. Object-related expectations appear in tasks that do not require the same motor demands as manual search.

During later infancy, perceptual specialization becomes increasingly pronounced. Joint attention emerges. Crawling changes the infant's physical relationship to space and therefore changes the information available to be learned. Statistical learning becomes increasingly flexible.

During the second year, symbolic representation expands. Language, imitation, social referencing, pretend play, and increasingly complex action plans allow the infant to participate in a richer world of shared meaning.

The sequence is neither a rigid timetable nor a random collection of milestones.

It is an interaction among maturation, experience, action, attention, memory, and relationships.

